15.3  Interfaces and routes

TL;DR
  - An interface is a network connection point: enp3s0 (cable), wlp2s0 (Wi-Fi),
    lo (the machine itself).
  - The routing table says where each packet goes: straight to a local network,
    or to the default gateway (your router) for everything else.
  - ip shows and changes both; what you change with it is lost at reboot.

      ip -br addr
      ip route
      ip route get 1.1.1.1     # which way a packet would take

On your machine
  docker0   down · virtual · MAC 02:42:5c:11:aa:01
            172.17.0.1/16
  enp0s31f6 down · MAC 54:e1:ad:12:34:56
  lo        unknown · loopback
            127.0.0.1/8, ::1/128
  wlp0s20f3 up · wireless · MAC 8c:c6:81:2a:10:7f
            192.168.1.23/24, fe80::8ec6:81ff:fe2a:107f/64
  Route     default via 192.168.1.1 on wlp0s20f3

Pitfalls
  - NetworkManager or systemd-networkd manage the network on most systems:
    change settings with nmcli or their files, or they undo your ip commands.
  - Names like enp3s0 come from the card's position in the machine; older guides
    say eth0.
  - With two default routes (Wi-Fi and cable, or a VPN), the lowest metric wins.
  - Docker, VPNs and VMs add their own interfaces and routes (docker0, tun0,
    virbr0): that's normal.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 15.3 --why

Related
  Commands  ip, nmcli
  Paths     /sys/class/net/, /proc/net/route
  Hub       idk network Network
  See also  idk 15.2 DNS resolution
